<!--{ da02 : rich }-->
# 7. Data tables

A **data table** holds values that other lines compute with, as a range of
cells does in a spreadsheet: a list of load cases, of sections, of prices.
About eight minutes.

Here is one, named `sections`:
<!--{ da03 : data }-->
<% sections: stilo.dataframe {
    columns: ["Section", "h (mm)", "Mass (kg/m)"],
    rows: [
        ["IPE 240", 240, 30.7],
        ["IPE 300", 300, 42.2],
        ["IPE 330", 330, 49.1]
    ]
} %>
<!--{ da04 : markdown }-->
The lines below use it by its name. The heaviest section weighs
**<% math.max (list.enum (sections.column "Mass (kg/m)")) %> kg/m**, and the
first row is <% list.enum (sections.row 0) %>.

| You write | You get |
|-----------|---------|
| `sections.column "Mass (kg/m)"` | the values of a column, as a list |
| `sections.row 0` | a row, as a list; rows count from 0 |
| `sections.columns` | the column names, as a list |
| `sections.rows` | all the rows, as a list of lists |

`list.enum` turns a list into separate values, which `math.max` compares.

Press **Enter** on the table to edit it: a **Name**, the column names in the
first row, and one value in every other field. A value is written as a
formula without its brackets: a number, `120`; a text, in double quotes,
`"ULS-1"`; or a calculation, `1.35 * 100`. Press **Escape** to leave it.
<!--{ da05 : markdown }-->
## Your turn

1. Select the empty line just above the first check below and press **d**:
   a new data table.
2. In **Name**, write `loads`.
3. Write the column names `Case` and `Fx (kN)` in the first row.
4. Fill in three rows, with **Tab** to go to the next field (Tab in the last
   field adds a row): `"ULS-1"` and `120`, `"ULS-2"` and `1.35 * 100`,
   `"SLS"` and `90`. A text without its quotes is outlined in red: add them.
5. Press **Ctrl+Enter**.
<!--{ da06 : rich }-->

<!--{ da07 : formula }-->
<% tutorial.check (list.find "Fx (kN)" loads.columns >= 0) %>
<!--{ da08 : formula }-->
<% tutorial.check (list.size loads.rows >= 3) %>
<!--{ da09 : markdown }-->
Now compute with your table. Select the empty line just above the next check,
press **=**, and write
`max_fx = math.max (list.enum (loads.column "Fx (kN)"))`.
<!--{ da10 : rich }-->

<!--{ da11 : formula }-->
<% tutorial.check ((type max_fx == "Numb") & (max_fx == math.max (list.enum (loads.column "Fx (kN)")))) %>
<!--{ da12 : markdown }-->
The largest horizontal load is **<% max_fx ; "…" %> kN**.
<!--{ da15 : markdown }-->
## Charts

A **chart** draws the values of a data table. This one shows the mass of
the sections above against their height:
<!--{ da16 : chart }-->
<% stilo.charts.line {
    x: sections.column "h (mm)",
    y: sections.column "Mass (kg/m)",
    x_label: "h (mm)",
    y_label: "Mass (kg/m)",
    title: "IPE sections"
} %>
<!--{ da17 : markdown }-->
Press **Enter** on it: it is edited as a form. **Chart type** chooses the
kind of chart (only Line for now). **X values** and **Y values** are formulas
that give a list of numbers, usually a column of a table, such as
`sections.column "h (mm)"`. The labels and the title are plain text, without
quotes, and can stay empty. **Tab** goes to the next field, **Ctrl+Enter**
saves.

**Try it:** select the empty line below and press **c**: a new chart. In
**X values** write `sections.column "h (mm)"`, in **Y values**
`sections.column "Mass (kg/m)"`, give it a title, and press **Ctrl+Enter**.
<!--{ da18 : rich }-->

<!--{ da13 : rich }-->
## The Stilo library

Data tables and charts need the **Stilo library**, a document of functions that comes
with Stilo. When you write the first data table or chart of a document, the editor adds
a hidden line at the top that imports it. This lesson has that line already.
To see it, open the commands (**F1**) and choose **Show or hide the hidden
cells**; choose it again to hide it.

The library is found in the *project* the document belongs to. This tutorial
is a project; lesson 12 shows how to make your own folder one.

More in the manual: [Data table cells](https://www.onlabs.org/manual/editor/cell-types.html#data-table-cells).
